tr=''
for r,label in zip(rows,['0.5% strict sizing','One-pair control','1% capped one-pair pilot']):
 rec=r['reconciliation'];s=r['statistics'];n=rec['episodes'] if rec else 0;pnl=rec['net_pnl'] if rec else 0
 tr+=f'<tr><td><a href="{url(r)}">{label} ↗</a></td><td>{n}</td><td>{fmt(pnl)}</td><td>{s["Net Profit"]}</td><td>{s["Drawdown"]}</td><td>{str(rec["wins"])+" / "+str(n) if rec else "—"}</td></tr>'
run_table='<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Completed QC run</th><th>Strangles</th><th>Net P&amp;L</th><th>Account return</th><th>Account drawdown</th><th>Winning pairs</th></tr></thead><tbody>'+tr+'</tbody></table></div>'
auditrows=''
for name in ['TLT','CRWD','META']:
 a=strict['runtime'];v=[float(x) for x in a['audit_'+name+'_stress'].split(',')];n=a['audit_'+name+'_n']
 auditrows+=f'<tr><td>{name}</td><td>{n}</td><td>{fmt(v[0])}</td><td>{fmt(v[1])}</td><td>{fmt(v[3])}</td><td>{fmt(v[0]/.005)}</td></tr>'
audit_table='<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Sampled decisions</th><th>Minimum stress</th><th>Median stress</th><th>Maximum stress</th><th>Equity needed for smallest pair at 0.5%</th></tr></thead><tbody>'+auditrows+'</tbody></table></div>'
p=pilot['reconciliation'];c=control['reconciliation'];pr=pilot['runtime']
worst=min(c['episode_summary'],key=lambda e:e['net_pnl'])
body=f'''<div class="eyebrow">QC / completed cloud evidence / 25 Oct 2026</div><h1>Automation works.<br>The edge is unproven.</h1><p class="lead">The corrected-cost control made {fmt(c['net_pnl'])} on a $150,000 account. A simpler capped pilot made {fmt(p['net_pnl'])}. These are completed, reconciled backtests—not live results or a claim of optimal rules.</p>
<h2 id="runs">Three tests, different questions</h2><p>2 Jan 2024–23 Oct 2026 · CRWD, EWZ, META, TLT and SWKS · $150,000 initial equity. Click a run to open its QC result; login may be required.</p>{run_table}
<p>The strict run asks whether the earlier 0.5% stress budget fits one contract. The control disables stress sizing and stress/loss exits to test the trading engine. The pilot restores those controls at a proposed 1% per-trade budget with one pair maximum. Differences are not a causal estimate of sizing quality.</p>
<div class="notice"><strong>Small, selected sample.</strong> The pilot thresholds were chosen after inspecting this same period’s rejection diagnostics. This is an in-sample feasibility test, not an independent validation or a reason to favor TLT in future.</div>
<h2 id="cost">The cost correction is verified in actual fills</h2><p>Final runs use <strong>$1 per contract per order</strong> and <strong>$0.01 per share of adverse slippage</strong>, on top of bid/ask market fills. For a standard 100-share contract this adds $1 slippage per leg execution. The earlier V2 model charged <em>1% of premium</em> instead. Its −$127.44 became +$293.00 in V4 with the same 100 order identities and timestamps: the $420.44 difference is a cost-model change.</p>
<p>The final control and pilot use a positive standalone margin reserve, fixing the signed-QC-margin comparison. The pilot measures stress loss from the original net entry credit throughout each trade. Earlier results and source versions remain archived.</p>
<h2 id="sizing">Why the old sizing rejects every trade</h2><p>0.5% × $150,000 = <strong>$750</strong>. Quantity was floor($750 / stress loss per pair). All <strong>479 sizing attempts</strong> returned zero. Repeated minute attempts are not independent opportunities; the diagnostic sampled the first eligible attempt for each symbol/date/expiry: <strong>47 observations</strong>.</p>{audit_table}
<p>TLT’s ±20% intrinsic-value loss floor alone was $1,179–$1,432 after credit and estimated closing fees. It already exceeded $750 before adding volatility/time value. The rejection is an economic constraint, not a volatility-unit or multiplier mistake. EWZ and SWKS never reached the sizing stage under these filters; no stress estimate is inferred for them.</p>
<p>Grid: spot −20%, −10%, 0%, +10%, +20%; IV +0/+10/+20 volatility points; time 0, 1 and days to 21 DTE. Full BSM repricing, actual multipliers and costs. This grid is a chosen stress, not a calibrated percentile or a worst-case loss bound. Required equity above is historical arithmetic, not permission for a future entry.</p>
<h2 id="pilot">What the proposed pilot actually did</h2><p>{p['episodes']} closed pairs; {p['wins']} winners; {fmt(p['fees'])} fees. All entries were TLT. {pr.get('ss_exit_21dte','0')} exits were scheduled; {pr.get('ss_exit_stress','0')} were early stress exits. Check the <a href="pilot_trades.csv">trade ledger</a> for the complete outcomes. The rule is operationally executable but has not established an attractive return.</p>
<p>The control’s largest realized pair loss was {fmt(worst['net_pnl'])} on {worst['name']}. Its pair win rate is {c['wins']}/{c['episodes']}, not QC’s separate leg-level win rate. Low account returns also reflect the fixed one-pair scale; that does not justify increasing exposure.</p>
<h2 id="limits">What these runs do not establish</h2><ul><li><strong>Exact 45/21 timing:</strong> entry allows 40–50 calendar DTE; holiday/half-day exits move earlier; risk and earnings exits are permitted. New entries stop 35 calendar days before the sample ends.</li><li><strong>Full earnings visibility:</strong> QC’s <a href="https://www.quantconnect.com/docs/v2/writing-algorithms/datasets/eod-historical-data/upcoming-earnings">upcoming-earnings universe</a> exposes seven days. It cannot prove the entire planned holding window was known event-free at entry.</li><li><strong>Live replication:</strong> marketable combo fills differ from your limit-order patience. Quotes, assignment, margin and stress behavior still need broker/paper validation.</li><li><strong>Model precision:</strong> IVR uses an approximate ATM pair near 30 DTE. Delta/stress use BSM, 4% interest and zero dividend yield; American exercise and dividends are not fully represented by this approximation.</li><li><strong>Optimality:</strong> no held-out sample, 21-DTE versus expiration comparison, or profitability optimization was performed. The five-name universe was inherited from prior research.</li></ul>
<h2>Reproducible receipts</h2><p><a href="qc_evidence.json">Completed results, runtime diagnostics, source hashes and order reconciliation (JSON)</a> · <a href="control_trades.csv">Control trade ledger</a> · <a href="pilot_trades.csv">Pilot trade ledger</a> · <a href="onepair_cost_v6.py.txt">Final control source</a> · <a href="pilot_onepair_v6.py.txt">Final pilot source</a>.</p><p>All final order cashflows reconcile to equity, with no remaining contracts or unmatched fills. Twelve local tests cover units, signed margin, entry cost basis and lifecycle recovery. Ordinary cloud fills do not prove every exceptional recovery path.</p><p><a href="sizing.html">Follow the simple sizing checklist ↗</a> · <a href="evidence.html">Provider context and the rule ledger ↗</a></p>'''
(W/'qc.html').write_text(page('Completed QC tests',body,'evidence.html'))
body='''<div class="eyebrow">Position size / proposed paper pilot</div><h1>One pair.<br>Or skip.</h1><p class="lead">Start with one short put and one short call, same expiry. Never round a fractional capacity up. Do not increase contracts just because the premium looks attractive.</p>
<div class="notice"><strong>Proposed limits, not approved live settings.</strong> This pilot doubles the earlier illustrative trade stress allowance from 0.5% to 1% of account equity. If you want to retain 0.5%, keep skipping pairs that do not fit. The backtest establishes feasibility, not a profitable edge. <a href="qc.html#pilot">See the tested result ↗</a></div>
<div class="formula">Trade 1 pair only if every check passes.<br>Otherwise trade 0. Never add a second pair to the same underlying.</div>
<h2>Before entry: five checks</h2><ol><li><strong>One-pair stress loss ≤1% of net liquidation value.</strong> Use fresh leg quotes and the saved scenario grid below. Include both legs, multiplier, fees and estimated closing costs.</li><li><strong>Combined stress stays within the book limits.</strong> All SS45DTE positions together ≤2% of equity; each correlated group ≤1%. Sum standalone modeled losses without diversification credit. CRWD/META/SWKS share a technology group.</li><li><strong>Margin leaves room.</strong> The new pair’s positive margin reserve ≤5% of equity; total account margin usage after entry ≤20%. Check the broker’s actual order preview; a low margin number is not a loss limit.</li><li><strong>At most two SS45DTE pairs open.</strong> At most one pair per underlying. Unrelated holdings must also be reviewed for correlated risk; the backtest account had no outside holdings.</li><li><strong>The entry checklist passes.</strong> Monthly expiry 40–50 DTE, IVR >50, |delta| 0.16–0.22 per leg, OI >500, positive fresh bids and spread &lt;5% of midpoint. Verify the full planned earnings window yourself.</li></ol>
<h2>Example: a $150,000 account</h2><p>Trade/cluster stress ceiling: <strong>$1,500</strong>. Total strategy stress ceiling: <strong>$3,000</strong>. New-pair margin ceiling: <strong>$7,500</strong>; total account margin ceiling: <strong>$30,000</strong>.</p><p>A hypothetical pair with $1,400 stress loss and $3,000 margin can fit if the book has capacity. A pair with $2,200 stress loss cannot. Quantity stays <strong>one</strong> even if the arithmetic suggests more. Historical TLT sampled stress was $1,303–$1,444; a new trade needs a new valuation. <a href="qc.html#sizing">See the measured ranges ↗</a></p>
<h2>Calculate stress consistently</h2><p>Reprice both legs at spot moves −20%, −10%, 0%, +10%, +20%; IV increases 0, 10, 20 volatility points; and elapsed time 0, 1 and the remaining days to the 21-DTE exit. Take the largest modeled buyback loss, including costs. The grid is a research convention and can miss larger moves.</p><div class="formula">Stress loss per pair = max across scenarios<br>(modeled closing cost − original net entry credit)</div><p>Use original net entry credit during the entire trade; do not reset the loss basis to today’s midpoint. For multiple existing pairs, normalize cashflows by quantity before applying the multiplier once. A broker risk tool or the documented consumer pricing model can do this; do not estimate it from premium or margin alone.</p>
<h2 id="manage">During the trade</h2><ul><li><strong>Review near the open and before the close.</strong> Recheck events, quotes, cumulative P&amp;L, scenario loss and account capacity.</li><li><strong>Hard exits:</strong> close by 21 DTE; close/reduce if modeled loss or actual marked loss exceeds the 1% trade limit, group/book stress exceeds its limit, or account margin exceeds 20%. Move holiday/half-day exits to the previous full session.</li><li><strong>Drawdown halt:</strong> at 10% below the account’s running peak, close strategy exposure and stop new entries pending review. This is a trial convention, not a guaranteed drawdown ceiling.</li><li><strong>Discretionary alerts:</strong> |delta| >0.35 and material news prompt a recorded hold/reduce/close decision. They cannot postpone a hard exit. No rescue size or rescue rolling.</li><li><strong>After confirmed closure:</strong> reassess the next monthly expiry. Same-day replacement is permitted only after both old legs are flat and every new-entry check passes.</li></ul>
<div class="notice green"><strong>If 0.5% is your intended risk allowance:</strong> retain the $750 ceiling on a $150,000 account. The tested naked pairs do not fit. Skip, or separately evaluate a defined-risk iron condor using max(put-wing width, call-wing width) × multiplier − net credit + closing costs, with one lot only if that amount fits. That is a different strategy and was not backtested here.</div>
<p class="small">Naked-call losses are theoretically unlimited. A 1% modeled stress budget and exit instruction do not cap realized losses at 1%; gaps, assignment and poor liquidity can exceed it. Missing current quotes, broker capacity or credible stress valuation means skip new exposure. The proposal is for paper testing until independent validation supports live use.</p><p><a href="qc.html">Completed QC evidence and trade ledgers ↗</a></p>'''
(W/'sizing.html').write_text(page('One pair or skip',body,'sizing.html'))
# Replace stale status on the main guide while retaining its six-step layout.
